+Liveness was verified (inverting the varint roundtrip assertion produced a shrunk
+counterexample). A 10x exploratory pass (`test_cases = 1000`) on every non-stream
+property found no additional failures.
+
+Note on running the suite: the shared machine is under heavy memory pressure
+(disk 100% full, ~700 MB RAM free across many workspace targets). The default
+fully-parallel `cargo test` gets OOM-killed (SIGKILL) partway through unrelated
+workspace crates; running `-p quinn-proto --lib -- --test-threads=1` (or 2)
+completes cleanly. This is an environment constraint, not a test defect.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### `RangeSet::replace` corrupts the set on an empty range
+
+**Minimal counterexample:** `x = 0` — `RangeSet::new().replace(0..0)`.
+
+**Symptom:** after `replace(x..x)` on an empty set, the set contains a degenerate
+`x..x` entry: `is_empty()` returns `false`, `min()`/`peek_min()` report a phantom
+`x..x` range, even though the set holds no elements.
+
+**Why it's a bug:** `RangeSet::insert` and `RangeSet::remove` both begin with
+`if x.is_empty() { return false; }`, i.e. an empty input range is a documented
+no-op and the set is meant never to hold empty ranges. `replace` has no such
+guard. Its `Replace` iterator's `Drop` impl unconditionally does
+`self.set.0.insert(self.range.start, self.range.end)`, so an empty input range is
+stored verbatim as `start == end`.
+
+**Code path at fault:** `quinn-proto/src/range_set/btree_range_set.rs`
+— `RangeSet::replace` (no empty-range guard) and `impl Drop for Replace`
+(`self.set.0.insert(self.range.start, self.range.end)`).
+
+**Production reachability:** `Assembler::insert`
+(`quinn-proto/src/connection/assembler.rs:165`) calls
+`recvd.replace(offset..offset + bytes.len() as u64)` while the stream is in
+unordered-read mode (`State::Unordered`, entered via `ensure_ordering(false)`).
+A STREAM frame carrying no data (`bytes.len() == 0` — e.g. an empty FIN-only
+frame) makes this an empty-range `replace`, storing a bogus entry in the
+`recvd` set. The immediate consequence is that `recvd` misreports emptiness/min;
+whether this escalates to observable misbehaviour in the assembler would need
+follow-up, but the `RangeSet`-level invariant violation is unambiguous and is
+covered by the failing regression test. Per the task instructions the bug was
+**not** fixed and the test is left failing.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright, but one generator was **narrowed** after investigation:
+
+- `partial_decode_accounts_for_every_byte` initially drew `cid_len` up to
+  `2 * MAX_CID_SIZE`. That produced a failure: `cid_len = 21` trips
+  `debug_assert!(len <= MAX_CID_SIZE)` in `ConnectionId::from_buf`
+  (`shared.rs:90`). Investigation showed this is **outside the parser's domain**,
+  not a bug: the local CID length is chosen by one's own endpoint and is always
+  `<= MAX_CID_SIZE`, and the crate's own fuzz harness
+  (`fuzz/fuzz_targets/packet.rs`) bounds `local_cid_len` to `0..=MAX_CID_SIZE`
+  identically. The generator was constrained to match, with a comment.
+
+- The `replace` rule in the range-set model test excludes empty ranges (the
+  distinct empty-range bug above is covered by a dedicated failing test), so the
+  model can explore the rest of the API in depth rather than shrinking to that
+  one shallow failure on step 1. This is the "keep a separate test for the
+  extreme" pattern from the skill's Common Mistakes.
+
+- Range values in the range-set model test are bounded to `0..=1000`. This bound
+  protects the **test's** memory (the element-wise `BTreeSet` model and the
+  `elts()`/well-formedness checks would materialize up to 2^62 elements and OOM),
+  **not** the libraries' contract. The full-width u64 behaviour of range handling
+  is exercised indirectly by the ACK-frame roundtrip test, whose ranges span the
+  whole `VarInt` space. An earlier version of this test did OOM-kill the process
+  before I added a cheap per-range well-formedness check in the invariant that
+  fails fast instead of materializing elements.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked well and the Rust reference was accurate — the stateful
+testing API, `#[hegel::composite]`, `one_of!`, `sampled_from`, `arrays`, and the
+generator/combinator surface all matched reality on `hegeltest 0.28.2`, and the
+tests compiled against the documented API with only trivial fixes. Specific
+points:
+
+1. **`rand` extra version mismatch is real and worth flagging louder.** The
+   `extras.md` note says hegel uses rand 0.9 and that rand 0.8 is incompatible.
+   `quinn-proto` pins **rand 0.10** (`rand = "0.10.1"` in `Cargo.toml`), which is
+   *newer* than hegel's 0.9. Several APIs in the crate under test are 0.10-only
+   (`RngExt`, `random_range`, `TryRng`). The `rand` extra was therefore unusable
+   here. The guidance is framed entirely around "project uses an *older* rand";
+   it should also cover "project uses a *newer* rand than hegel," where the
+   integration simply cannot be used and the right move is to test RNG-consuming
+   code paths through their non-RNG surface (as I did — transport-parameter
+   roundtrips generate the grease parameter / write order deterministically
+   rather than via `randoms()`). This was easy to work around but the reference
+   implies rand support is a solved thing.
+
+2. **Memory-footprint guidance for stateful model tests could be stronger.**
+   Common Mistake #7 mentions oracles that "materialize what the library
+   represents lazily" can OOM, which is exactly what bit me: an element-wise
+   `BTreeSet` model plus `elts()` collection of a range set OOM-killed the whole
+   process (not a panic — a SIGKILL) on a range like `0..2^62`. The stateful
+   section would benefit from a concrete "if your model or invariant enumerates
+   elements, bound the drawn magnitudes and add a cheap structural check that
+   fails before enumeration" note, since a SIGKILL in a stateful run looks
+   identical to the "shrinking takes minutes" hang the section already warns
+   about, and the two need opposite responses.
+
+3. **`draw` requires `&TestCase` in helper functions but the state-machine rule
+   signature hands you `TestCase` by value.** Minor, but when factoring a shared
+   `draw_range(tc: &TestCase)` helper out of several rules, it's not obvious from
+   the reference whether to pass `TestCase` or `&TestCase`; `&TestCase` works
+   because `draw` takes `&self`. A one-line note that `TestCase` methods take
+   `&self` (so helpers should borrow) would save a compile-cycle.
+
+4. **The reference is silent on `ResetToken`/fixed-size array field generators via
+   `arrays`.** I used `generators::arrays(generators::integers::<u8>())` with a
+   `[u8; N]` annotation for reset tokens and grease payloads; this worked exactly
+   as the `arrays` docs describe, including inferring `N`. No problem — just
+   confirming the documented turbofish caveat (`arrays::<i32, 5>` is E0107) is
+   correct and the annotation-driven form is the way to go.
+
+5. **Nothing in the skill was wrong or blocking.** The workflow's insistence on
+   grounding properties in the fuzz targets was the single most useful piece of
+   guidance for this crate — `fuzz/fuzz_targets/{packet,params,streamid,streams}.rs`
+   told me precisely which objects the maintainers consider worth generating and
+   what invariants they already check, and three of the tests are direct ports.
